{
  "schema_version": 2,
  "notes": [
    "Sample metric_definitions.json - the catalogue of everything the collector knows how to measure. Copy to metric_definitions.json and edit. The shipped file carries around ninety metrics across SQL Server, Oracle, PostgreSQL and MySQL; this sample keeps four, one of each shape, so the structure is readable.",
    "'metric_code' is engine-neutral and is the identity of the measurement everywhere else: reports, SLA policies, per-instance overrides and severity maps all name it. Prefix by theme (INSTANCE_, BACKUP_, STORAGE_, OS_, SECURITY_, MAINTENANCE_) so a new metric sorts with its family.",
    "'db_type': 'multi' means the metric exists for several engines and the 'variants' list says how it is obtained on each. A metric with no variant for an engine is simply not collected there - that is a statement, not a gap to fill with a wrong query.",
    "'variants' bind one metric to one SQL file per engine and version range. 'min_major_version'/'max_major_version' are how an old engine gets a query it can actually parse instead of failing every cycle; the file paths are relative to assets/metrics/.",
    "'collector_type' is 'sql' (a query against the instance), 'cmd' (a command on the machine, over SSH or WinRM using the target's cmd_access), or 'docker'. A cmd metric on a target with no cmd_access is skipped, not failed.",
    "'default_importance' is 0-5 and is the baseline weight of this metric everywhere. Per-instance exceptions belong in metric_importance_overrides.json, not here - this file describes the metric, that one describes your estate.",
    "'connection_error_severity' and 'execution_error_severity' say what it MEANS when the metric cannot run at all. For an availability check, failing to connect is the finding; for a housekeeping check it usually is not.",
    "'time_window' carries this metric's own schedule inside the collection pass. An expensive metric (index fragmentation, CHECKDB history) is confined to night hours and given a long timeout; a cheap one runs every minute. Keep every timeout here below the pass timeout in app_commands.json.",
    "'max_rows' caps what one metric may return, so a pathological result set cannot fill the store or a message."
  ],
  "collection": {
    "max_parallel_servers": 16,
    "note": "How many server_id groups a pass may walk at once. Metrics of ONE server_id always run one at a time, in catalogue order, so a machine never gets two sessions at once; only different machines overlap. Set 1 for a fully serial pass while debugging."
  },
  "metrics": [
